[{:db/id "q-117952/selection/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" :answer/id #uuid "fe6674f7-c4fa-527d-9e15-f9e0525a04c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117952/selection/c9ec5d6cdf4e67c7f8bce68475bed400e56bf403f3d09b5928f78b6baa512eab" :answer/id #uuid "f6118fe7-4474-5234-8b31-d3c179e5f81d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117952/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "00e1e26c-c479-5d2a-8667-cffcdf2bae7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117952/selection/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3" :answer/id #uuid "c70e97ee-f4c4-54fc-a2af-0fc918960ac6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117952/selection/2fa5baf88b61736a3022c72e24e172fda2b4270fffff6efaa1fc03fef23fccc2" :answer/id #uuid "5d38268d-94ea-5f6e-ba2f-f6824f24ba41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117952/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f04d5f3-56b7-568c-9133-da2970e11975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117952/selection/2fa5baf88b61736a3022c72e24e172fda2b4270fffff6efaa1fc03fef23fccc2" :answer-field/choices ["q-117952/selection/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" "q-117952/selection/c9ec5d6cdf4e67c7f8bce68475bed400e56bf403f3d09b5928f78b6baa512eab" "q-117952/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" "q-117952/selection/ebe3072a1dd0240f82f7c04e06603ef971401ed1aea6a421c38664ef5adb09f3" "q-117952/selection/2fa5baf88b61736a3022c72e24e172fda2b4270fffff6efaa1fc03fef23fccc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117952" :question/id #uuid "f6499313-4d81-5fcc-b371-1967baa7ac33" :question/math-academy-id "q-117952" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={1}^{2}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={2}^{2}-{1}^{2}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={5}^{2}-{2}^{2}=21 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=21\\cdot 1=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+21=26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $21$ | $21$ |\n| $3$ | $26$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈26.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117952/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117952"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "c0ef02c4-d176-5303-bea9-74e7801ea057" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "9c33a8d1-6695-5509-aab6-46178d326bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "8581bd9f-fb32-5ab3-a5a4-c58efa72d4dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897/selection/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf" :answer/id #uuid "1d0e9fc1-0a12-54c4-a295-c009de336e66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "46aaf540-e1be-53d0-9453-009c393d7844" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3facde-2447-5e25-aa34-70989ba869df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120897/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-120897/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-120897/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-120897/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-120897/selection/04ab8c59d12921ca2d995f79fd8be713086563c65c468afaa0d4bd8ea5bfffaf" "q-120897/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120897" :question/id #uuid "4605d331-af4a-5e7d-95f1-ab082035677a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120897" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120897/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120897"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911/selection/25d79eef8a16f080073a8e877263e916b245aa7f9e3282b422f7b7d7005baf31" :answer/id #uuid "d3d4a227-7dd7-52c2-922c-abdb97d7e09f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "343168a6-9bea-5a8b-b93e-6fad183c2369" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" :answer/id #uuid "b061778a-a743-5ff9-9252-9b3be6b71f12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "c3ff9326-e400-5906-a666-ad7952d7294c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e" :answer/id #uuid "75e8bd09-f6ed-5ce3-9fc9-c75e4df729ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8faec8c-9f54-54c5-9845-6f22ae418827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120911/selection/25d79eef8a16f080073a8e877263e916b245aa7f9e3282b422f7b7d7005baf31" :answer-field/choices ["q-120911/selection/25d79eef8a16f080073a8e877263e916b245aa7f9e3282b422f7b7d7005baf31" "q-120911/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-120911/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" "q-120911/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-120911/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120911" :question/id #uuid "13b68e5e-22d6-5fa0-a4a4-6c5d1f4c0a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-120911" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=1\\cdot \\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $2+2\\sqrt{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2+2\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120911/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120911"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "451f4184-9d29-5572-968f-afce92bc545a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "b38b6c1d-d210-5552-a098-f5f8e517569b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "ba898944-133e-563c-a171-a7991ea5cace" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "fc0d4586-af93-58fc-867a-97e13be4ea22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "269f2e0e-36ac-5686-89b3-2f87ae2b352c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "10075792-0bbe-5597-a3ea-5475ca2e704f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120939/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-120939/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-120939/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-120939/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-120939/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-120939/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120939" :question/id #uuid "63e5ecc0-7b49-5db6-b8e0-4e8470f5565a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120939" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=0+(-1)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y={1}^{2}+(-2)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120939/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120939"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
